  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e/4$ | ${e}^{3}/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{e/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
